\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces The flow of an analysis in \texttt {fea\_engine}{} (blue: package code; grey: data; violet: \texttt {rom\_engine}{}; green: validation only; dashed: optional). The link to \texttt {rom\_engine}{} is plain arrays, never an import.\relax }}{3}{figure.caption.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces The element interface. The base class owns the Gauss loop; a subclass supplies the interpolation and overrides only what differs.\relax }}{4}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.3}{\ignorespaces \code {solve_static} time on a sparse plane-stress plate with \code {backend="scipy"} and \code {backend="auto"} (left), and the extra time of \code {auto} (right). Minimum of three runs on the sandbox used for the figures of this chapter (2 CPUs){}. The torch path was not run (torch is not installed\xspace {} in the sandbox), and none of the sizes exceeds the threshold at which \code {auto} would choose it.\relax }}{8}{figure.caption.13}%
\gdef \the@ipfilectr {}
\gdef \the@ipfilectr {@-3}
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Why a positive Jacobian matters. A convex element has $\qopname \relax m{det}\bm {J}>0$ at every Gauss point; a re-entrant corner makes it non-positive and the stiffness wrong without an error message.\relax }}{11}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces Four generated meshes: a hole off the centre of a rectangle with grading to a growth ratio (top left), a notch, a fillet, and a quarter plate with a hole. Produced by \texttt {scripts/ch02\_assets.py}.\relax }}{13}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces Left: the largest ratio of adjacent element sizes of a 20-element power-law partition against the exponent. Right: the partition for a target growth ratio of 1.2, against a uniform one.\relax }}{13}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.4}{\ignorespaces A multi-block mesh: a quadrilateral and a triangle block over one shared node array. The interface nodes (red) belong to both blocks.\relax }}{14}{figure.caption.23}%
\gdef \the@ipfilectr {}
\gdef \the@ipfilectr {@-4}
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces From the weak form to the matrices. The choices that decide whether an element is right are the interpolation, the material matrix and the quadrature rule.\relax }}{19}{figure.caption.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Lowest eigenvalue of the clamped unit-square membrane (\code {Quad4Laplace}), consistent against lumped mass: the relative error in absolute value. The consistent-mass eigenvalue is above the exact one and the lumped-mass eigenvalue is below it. Produced by \texttt {scripts/ch03\_assets.py}.\relax }}{20}{figure.caption.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces The mechanism of shear locking in a bilinear element under bending (schematic). The exact bending mode curves the edges and has no shear strain; the bilinear interpolation keeps the edges straight, which produces a parasitic shear strain and an artificially stiff element.\relax }}{20}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Pure bending of a plane-stress beam, tip deflection divided by the exact value, against the number of elements along the beam. The quadratic elements are exact on these meshes. The helper is the one used by \texttt {fea\_engine/tests/test\_benchmarks\_convergence.py}.\relax }}{21}{figure.caption.36}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces Convergence of the first cantilever frequency of the Euler--Bernoulli beam (left) and of the tip deflection of the Reissner beam (right) with the element size, with the observed order.\relax }}{23}{figure.caption.44}%
\gdef \the@ipfilectr {}
\gdef \the@ipfilectr {@-5}
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Newmark average acceleration: error against the exact solution of the discrete model (first mode of the 20-element cantilever, three periods) against the time step, with the observed order. Produced by \texttt {scripts/ch04\_assets.py}.\relax }}{28}{figure.caption.50}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Stability of the two integrators in terms of $\omega \Delta t$ for the highest frequency of the discrete system (schematic).\relax }}{28}{figure.caption.51}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Largest displacement against time at $0.9$ and $1.1$ times the critical step.\relax }}{29}{figure.caption.54}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Tip deflection of the cantilever under a step load, divided by the static value ($\zeta =1\%$): modal superposition with 1, 2 and 8 modes against all modes (left), and the largest error against all modes as a function of the number of modes retained (right).\relax }}{30}{figure.caption.56}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Tip receptance of the cantilever ($\zeta =1\%$ at modes 1 and 3): the direct complex solve and an independent modal sum coincide on the plotted scale.\relax }}{31}{figure.caption.58}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces Which dynamic solver for which problem (\Cref {tab:dy-choose} gives the same choice with costs).\relax }}{32}{figure.caption.60}%
\gdef \the@ipfilectr {}
\gdef \the@ipfilectr {@-6}
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces One Newton iteration on a scalar equilibrium curve (schematic): the tangent at $\bm {u}^0$ is followed to the load level, and the new iterate is the foot of that line.\relax }}{36}{figure.caption.65}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Force norm of a rigidly rotated unit-square element (a stress-free motion): linear kinematics against the total-Lagrangian element. Produced by \texttt {scripts/ch05\_assets.py}.\relax }}{37}{figure.caption.68}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces Stress--strain loop of \code {TrussPlastic2D} ($E=200$ GPa, $\sigma _y=250$ MPa, $H=5$ GPa): the element against the closed-form return map. Produced by \texttt {scripts/ch05\_assets.py}.\relax }}{39}{figure.caption.71}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces The von Mises truss: closed form \textup {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont (\ignorespaces \ref {eq:nl-vm}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}}, displacement control and arc length. Load control cannot pass the limit load. Produced by \texttt {scripts/ch05\_assets.py}; the problem is the one of \texttt {fea\_engine/examples/arc\_length\_snap\_through\_demo.py}.\relax }}{41}{figure.caption.76}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.5}{\ignorespaces Following a response with a limit point (schematic). Load control cannot pass the limit load; displacement control follows a snap-through but not a snap-back; arc length constrains the step length and makes the load factor an unknown.\relax }}{41}{figure.caption.77}%
\gdef \the@ipfilectr {}
\gdef \the@ipfilectr {@-7}
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Loop against batched assembly. The loop is the default and the reference; the batched path replaces the element loop by array contractions, in chunks, with a cached sparse pattern.\relax }}{48}{figure.caption.88}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Internal-force assembly of a \code {Quad4PlaneStressTL} plate: time of the loop and the batched route (left) and their ratio (right). Produced by \texttt {scripts/ch06\_assets.py}.\relax }}{50}{figure.caption.92}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.3}{\ignorespaces Wall time of the same load-controlled Newton solve with the default path and with batched assembly and a sparse tangent.\relax }}{50}{figure.caption.94}%
\gdef \the@ipfilectr {}
\gdef \the@ipfilectr {@-8}
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.1}{\ignorespaces The model problem on a $40\times 40$ mesh ($1521\xspace $ unknowns). Left: displacement with the contact set outlined; right: contact force $\bm {\lambda }$, which is zero outside the contact set. The solver finds $295\xspace $ active constraints in $12\xspace $ iterations. Produced by \texttt {scripts/ch07\_assets.py}.\relax }}{54}{figure.caption.99}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.2}{\ignorespaces Penalty against exact contact on the model problem ($841\xspace $ unknowns). Left: the largest penetration falls like $1/k_p$ but never reaches zero; the exact solution has no penetration (drawn at the plotting floor). Right: the condition number of the penalty matrix grows in proportion to $k_p$; the exact route solves a system whose condition number does not depend on any parameter.\relax }}{55}{figure.caption.100}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.3}{\ignorespaces Left: the complementarity condition (red) against a penalty law (blue, dashed), which lets the gap become negative. Right: the active-set iteration in four steps.\relax }}{56}{figure.caption.105}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.4}{\ignorespaces The active-set iteration on the model problem with $N=1521\xspace $ unknowns. Left: the number of active constraints per iteration for the \texttt {reduced} and \texttt {saddle} routes (the curves coincide). Right: the number of constraints that changed status; the last point (plotted at 0.5) is zero changes, which is the stopping test.\relax }}{57}{figure.caption.106}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.5}{\ignorespaces Wall time of the three routes against the number of unknowns (data of \Cref {tab:ct-scaling}).\relax }}{61}{figure.caption.118}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.6}{\ignorespaces Error against time step for the two schemes (data of \Cref {tab:ct-order}).\relax }}{62}{figure.caption.125}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.7}{\ignorespaces Bar impact, $\Delta t=0.002$. Left: wall force over the first $0.4$ time units. Right: energy relative to the initial energy over the whole run.\relax }}{63}{figure.caption.128}%
\gdef \the@ipfilectr {}
\gdef \the@ipfilectr {@-9}
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.1}{\ignorespaces Two projections of the same full-order solution onto the same subspace. The Euclidean projection is what the POD error \textup {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont (\ignorespaces \ref {eq:pod-ey}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}} bounds; the Galerkin solution minimises the energy-norm error instead, so the two points differ and so do the two errors. Schematic, not computed.\relax }}{70}{figure.caption.138}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.2}{\ignorespaces The offline--online pipeline of \Cref {lst:pod-run}. Blue: the full-order model; violet: the reduced-order model; grey: plain data; green: error measure; dashed: optional or validation only. Only the lift touches the full dimension $n$ online.\relax }}{71}{figure.caption.140}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.3}{\ignorespaces Left: normalised singular values of the 60\xspace training snapshots. Right: worst relative error on 40\xspace unseen parameters against the number of modes $r$, for the Euclidean projection error and for the Galerkin solve with Euclidean and with mass-weighted POD. Produced by \texttt {scripts/ch08\_assets.py}.\relax }}{73}{figure.caption.142}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.4}{\ignorespaces Median Galerkin state error on 40\xspace unseen parameters against the number of snapshots, optimal Latin hypercube against random sampling, five repetitions each.\relax }}{73}{figure.caption.143}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.5}{\ignorespaces Time per query: reduced solve, full solve with affine assembly, full solve with re-assembly, at two sizes of the beam. Logarithmic axis; single runs on the sandbox used for the figures of this chapter (2 CPUs).\relax }}{74}{figure.caption.145}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.6}{\ignorespaces Cumulative cost of $Q$ queries. The reduced model pays $T_{\mathrm {off}}$ first and less per query afterwards; the lines cross at the break-even $Q^\ast $. Schematic: measured values are in \Cref {tab:pod-cost}.\relax }}{75}{figure.caption.148}%
\gdef \the@ipfilectr {}
\gdef \the@ipfilectr {@-10}
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.1}{\ignorespaces Left: Hankel singular values of the pre-reduced model. Right: the a priori bound \textup {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont (\ignorespaces \ref {eq:bt-bound}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}} and the actual grid-maximum error against the retained order. The error is measured against the exact response of the pre-reduced model. Produced by \texttt {scripts/ch09\_assets.py}.\relax }}{83}{figure.caption.157}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.2}{\ignorespaces Error in the band $0.05$--$8\,\omega _1$ against the reduced state count: peak-relative (left) and mean relative (right).\relax }}{83}{figure.caption.158}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.3}{\ignorespaces Relative error against frequency for models of order $8$ on an extended band; the dotted line marks the upper end of the band of \Cref {tab:bt-compare}.\relax }}{84}{figure.caption.160}%
